Here's the complete migration from ASP.NET WebForms to ASP.NET Core 8 MVC:

## Migrated Code

```csharp
// ReportController.cs — ASP.NET Core 8
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.Text;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    private readonly ILogger<ReportController> _logger;

    public ReportController(ILogger<ReportController> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    // Replaces btnExport_Click
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);

        // No Response.Clear/Write/End needed — just return a result.
        // FileContentResult sets Content-Disposition and ContentType for you.
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    // Replaces btnRedirect_Click
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult ProcessAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Response.Redirect("/login", true) used Thread.Abort internally.
            // In ASP.NET Core, just return a redirect result — no abort, no exception.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No catch (ThreadAbortException) needed — it doesn't exist in .NET 8.
        // OperationCanceledException is the modern equivalent if you use
        // CancellationToken in ProcessRequest.
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Client disconnected or request was cancelled
            _logger.LogInformation("Request was cancelled");
            return StatusCode(499); // Client Closed Request
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() => /* ... */ "";
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) => _logger.LogError(ex, "Request failed");
}
```

## Key Changes & Pitfalls

| WebForms Pattern | ASP.NET Core Replacement | Pitfall to Avoid |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.Clear/Write/End` | Return `File(...)` result | Don't write to `Response.Body` manually then also return an `IActionResult` — pick one |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `return Redirect(url)` | The `true` (endResponse) param triggered `Thread.Abort`; in Core, just return — execution stops naturally |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Remove entirely, or catch `OperationCanceledException` | `ThreadAbortException` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8; don't reference it at all |
| `Response.AddHeader(...)` | Handled by `FileContentResult` | If you need custom headers, use `Response.Headers.Append()` **before** returning a result |
| Event handlers (`btnExport_Click`) | Controller actions with `[HttpPost]` | WebForms postback model doesn't exist; use form submissions or API calls |

## Critical Migration Notes

1. **`Response.End()` → just return.** In WebForms, `Response.End` called `Thread.Abort` internally to halt execution. In Core, returning an `IActionResult` ends the response naturally. There is no equivalent and none is needed.

2. **`ThreadAbortException` is gone.** The `catch (ThreadAbortException) { }` "swallow" pattern was a WebForms anti-pattern forced by `Response.Redirect(url, true)`. Delete these blocks entirely.

3. **Use `HttpContext.RequestAborted`** if `ProcessRequest()` does long-running work:
   ```csharp
   private void ProcessRequest(CancellationToken ct = default)
   {
       ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
       // ... pass ct through the full call chain
   }

   // In the action:
   ProcessRequest(HttpContext.RequestAborted);
   ```

4. **Don't forget `Dispose`.** If you use `CancellationTokenSource` for timeouts, always wrap in `using`:
   ```csharp
   using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
       HttpContext.RequestAborted);
   cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
   ProcessRequest(cts.Token);
   ```
